#===============================================================================
# GBM scRNA-seq ANALYSIS
# Dataset: GSE229779
#
# Script: 03_Dimensionality Reduction, Clustering and UMAP.R
#
# Purpose:
#   1. PCA: Compress genes into components.
#   2. Selecting PCAs: How many components to keep.
#   3. Neighborhood Graph: Connects similar cells.
#   4. Clustering: Groups cells into graph.
#   5. UMAP.
#==============================================================================


#============================================================================
1. Run PCA
#=============================================================================

## Purpose:

PCA is computed on the highly variable genes selected in the previos step of the workflow analysis.

## run the code:

gbm <-RunPCA(
gbm, features = VariableFeatures(gbm))

RunPCA: Computes principal components from the cell ×gene expression matrix —the core dimensionality-reduction step.
features = VariableFeatures(object = gbm)
Restricts PCA to the ~2,000 the highly variable genes, instead of all genes —keeps the signal and drops the noise.

#Result:
a new "pca"reduction is stored in gbm —by default, 40 components (PCs), each one a weighted combination of genes.

## Visualizing PCA:

Run the code:
DimPlot(gbm, reduction = "pca")

#Result:
the code visualizes seurat 'gbm' by pca 'reduction method', by the only first two pca components:
pca1(the fisrt most variable genes component) and pca2 (the second most variable genes component)
on x and y axis, and cannot vosualize the other calculated PCAs components.

#===============================================================================================================

2. Selecting PCAs: How many components to keep:

### First run: Visualizing the Elbowplot:

# Run the code:
ElbowPlot(gbm)

#Purpose:
Visualize the PCA components to detrmine the cuttoff point where adding another PCs stop explaining
much more variance,
Everything before it is signal; everything after tends to be noise.
# Biologically: the early PCs usually track real cell-type differences; later PCs often reflect technical noise.

#Result:
The curve is flatten after around 11 Pc, which no adds any meaningful biological information,
and the tail represents noise data.

#### Second Run: Visualizing the heatmap for the selected PCAs components:

#Purpose:
Visualize the contributed up/down regulated most variable genes across the cells of the meaningful 11 selected PCs.

#Run the code:
DimHeatmap(gbm, dims = 1:11, cells = 500, balanced = TRUE)

DimHeatmap is the function for seurat project (gbm), picking 11 PCs, selecting 500 cells for better visualization,
balanced = True, between the high and low numbers of cells.

## Result:
elven heatmap figures for the 11 selected PCs, rows are the genes splitting between
between yellow (low expressed genes) and purple (highly expressed genes) across cells.

#=========================================================================================================================

3. Constructing the Neighbor Grapph:

# Purpose:
Build the KNN grapph from each group of neighbor cells, based on cell - cell distance from the 11 PCs.

# Run the code:

gbm <- FindNeighbors(gbm, dim = 1:11)

apply FindNeighbor function to compute each cell's nearest neighbors in the PCs space
for gbm seurate data on the kept 11 PCs determined from the cut off threshold of the elbow plot.

# Result:
KNN graph built for the next steps of the workflow.

#===================================================================================================================

4. Cell Clustering:

# Purpose:
Cluster Cells into groups after dimensionality reduction
based on the built KNN graph using specific mathematical algorithm (louvian).

# Run the code:

gbm <- FindClusters(gbm, resultion = 0.5)

Apply the function FindClusters to build cells clusters based on (Louvian) algorithm
to the neighbor graph, with 0.5 moderate resultion to get sub-cell types.

## Result:
Clusters of the cell groups based on the shared gene expression patterns.

#======================================================================================================================================

5. UMAP:

# Purpose:

UMAP reduction method can capture the non-linear relation between the cells, PCA cannot,
apply the non linear UMAP reduction method to visualize the clustered cell groups.

## First run the code:

gbm <- RunUMAP(gbm, dim = 1:11)

RunUMAP()computes a 2D UMAP embedding from the chosen PCs and
stores it as a new "umap"reduction in gbm.

dims = 1:11 - the same selected PCs used for FindNeighbors() and FindClusters().

## Second run the code:

DimPlot (gbm, reduction = "umap", label = TRUE)

DimPlot plots the clusters into two dimension, reduction method = umap.

### Result:

UMAP capture the nonlinear relation between cells
cell groups visulaized in 2D.
